german chancellor angela merkel has held  talks with ukrainian president vladimir  zelinsky in kiev the two discussed an  end to the conflict with russian-backed  separatists in eastern ukraine  germany is a key ally of ukraine's but  there are also tensions over the north  stream two gas pipeline  kiev is strongly opposed to the route  which will ship gas directly from russia  to germany depriving ukraine of both  transit fees and geopolitical leverage  and potentially allowing russia to cut  off ukraine's gas while keeping western  europe supplied  16 years dozens of bilateral visits and  four ukrainian presidents  angela merkel is certainly no stranger  to this part of the world  she came to office just after the first  maidan revolution swept a new president  to power but ukraine only really came to  dominate germany's diplomatic agenda in  2014  when pro-european protesters once again  took to kiev's maidan square and brought  another pro-russian government down  soon former president viktor yanukovych  fled to moscow and the eu and russia  were on a collision course  within weeks russia annexed ukraine's  crimean peninsula and began supporting  pro-russian separatists in the donbass  region  full-scale open warfare in europe was a  reality for the first time since the  balkan wars of the 1990s thousands lost  their lives and hundreds of thousands of  people were forced to flee their homes  negotiations late into the night and  countless calls between kiev moscow  brussels and washington angela merkel  was at the forefront of the shuttle  diplomacy that prevented a local  conflict from escalating  she was also determined to make russia  pay for flouting international law in  ukraine  most analysts are certain without  merkel's lobbying eu sanctions against  russia would never have been passed  but seeing from kiev this was also a  time of mixed messages from germany  just months after the last tank battle  in donbass in june 2015 nordstrom 2 was  announced  a further gas pipeline linking russia  and germany through the baltic sea would  allow the kremlin to completely bypass  ukraine's pipelines  not only would ukraine lose billions in  annual transit fees but also a key  deterrent to further russian military  intervention in ukraine ukraine's  leaders hoped that u.s pressure would  stop the project but when president joe  biden withdrew sanctions after a meeting  with merkel in july 2021 it was clear  that the battle was lost  a dream that ukraine's leaders haven't  given up on is nato membership or  something close to it  but angela merkel was never the leader  they could look to for support  she repeatedly vetoed nato plans to give  kiev any overt security guarantees let  alone membership  fear of antagonizing russia  as angela merkel makes her final visit  to ukraine the conflict in donbass is  still no closer to a negotiated  resolution  the german chancellor will be remembered  in kiev as a leader who made stabilizing  ukraine a priority but who also often  disappointed her ukrainian counterparts  by her refusal to push back harder  against russia  for more let's bring in dw's nick  connolly who is standing by in kiev  hello to you nick  so you were following the press  conference what did the two leaders say  well this was essentially about two  things this was about security and  ukraine's ongoing tensions with russia  security in donbass and about energy  that nordstrom 2 pipeline through the  baltic sea you mentioned and from  ukrainian perspective those two things  are intrinsically linked ukrainians  saying that if germany commissions this  pipeline then russia basically will have  a free hand to up the pressure on  ukraine to destabilize this country  because up until now they have been  dependent on security here in ukraine to  supply their customers and earn those  crucial billions from european customers  from angela merkel we heard a kind of  personal note of disappointment that  those negotiations year after year on  donbass through the mints format that  they hadn't achieved more you have to  say that russia has handed out about  half a million passports in that region  to people living there and is now  encouraging people living there with  russian passports to take part in  russian elections so no real sign that  those are going to come under ukrainian  control anytime soon and from vladimir  zelinski there was more of his criticism  of this pipeline he called it a weapon  that germany was needlessly basically  handing over to the russians he was  skeptical that the compensatory  mechanisms would work that ukraine would  get much in return and basically said  that the sanctions that are meant to  happen if russia does weaponize energy  were unenforceable so  very difficult to uh you know see any  closer positions there  well it was just two days ago that  angela merkel had a surprisingly warm  welcome from vladimir putin when she was  in moscow  just how welcome is the german  chancellor in kiev  well there were obviously warm words  from the host from vladimir zelensky and  recognition that angela merkel has  invested a huge amount of political  capital in ukraine down the years  especially in those last seven years  since russia annex crimea and began  supporting those separatists in donbass  but there was no overlooking those  tensions and you know the atmospherics  weren't particularly relaxed  voldemosilenski coming back to that  energy topic time and time again um  germany has said it will invest in  renewables here in ukraine to try and uh  dampen the blow from ukraine losing that  gas transit um and you would  try and prevent russia from weaponizing  energy but there was also talk of  military aid the ukrainians would like  germany to support ukraine's military as  the us and the uk are doing uh maybe  help rebuild its uh black sea fleet but  germany's staying very very quiet about  that no real willingness to provide  ukraine with lethal weapons and there's  a real disappointment here in kiev among  people who maybe are  expecting more from germany that germany  to their mind isn't willing to get off  the fence and take sides for ukraine and  is trying to you know basically keep its  roles a mediator between russia and  ukraine that's something that is  frustrating people here in ukraine as  this conflict continues  dw's nate connolly reporting for us from  kiev thanks so much  you
 

